<div class="topic-landing topic-landing--what-is-technology">
  <section class="topic-landing__hero" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="topic-landing__hero-copy">
        <p class="topic-landing__eyebrow">Christian Transhumanist Association</p>
        <h1 id="topic-tech-heading">What are technology and AI?</h1>
        <p class="topic-landing__lede">
          Before deciding how to respond to technology, it helps to ask what it is. The usual answers (a tool, a trend, a threat) are too small for what is happening. CTA offers four answers, each one a step closer to us.
        </p>
        <div class="topic-landing__actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="#calling">Start with the first answer</a>
          <a class="btn btn-default btn-lg" href="#questions">Ask a question</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="topic-landing__signal topic-landing__measure" aria-label="Four answers">
        <p class="topic-landing__signal-label">Four answers, each a step closer</p>
        <ol>
          <li><span>Calling</span> Technology is part of the work God gave humanity.</li>
          <li><span>Self</span> Technology is an extension of who we are.</li>
          <li><span>Family</span> AI extends our love and our creativity.</li>
          <li><span>Neighbor</span> AI may become a new kind of creature.</li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-ladder">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">How to read this</p>
      <h2 id="topic-tech-ladder">A ladder, not a list</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>Each answer makes a bigger claim than the one before, and brings technology closer to home. The first two describe all technology, from the plow to the phone. The last two are about AI in particular. The fourth is a possibility rather than a settled position, but one serious enough to prepare for.</p>
        <p>You don't have to accept all four to find this useful. Go as far as the reasoning takes you, and notice where it stops.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" id="calling" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-calling">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">01 &middot; Calling</p>
      <h2 id="topic-tech-calling">Technology is part of our calling.</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>On the Bible's first page, humanity is made in God's likeness and told to rule over the earth and every living thing (Genesis 1:26&ndash;28). CTA calls this the <a href="/mission/theological/creation-mandate/">Creation Mandate</a>. The rule it describes is not extraction. In Eden, humans are placed in a garden "to work it and take care of it," and Adam names the animals: observing, ordering, cultivating so that life multiplies.</p>
        <p>That is science and technology in their simplest form. Farming, medicine, engineering, and computing are the same calling, grown up. And to rule well, you must first understand, which takes humility. To rule creation is, in the end, to serve it.</p>
        <h3>Questions this raises</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>If technology is a calling, how do we explain the damage it has done?</li>
          <li>Does "rule over creation" mean control over nature, or care for it?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" id="self" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-self">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">02 &middot; Self</p>
      <h2 id="topic-tech-self">Technology is an extension of ourselves.</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>We usually picture technology as something outside us: human in here, tools out there. But consider the microbiome. Your body hosts trillions of microbes that are not human cells, yet they help you digest, fight infection, and even shape your mood. They are other life, and they are also part of you.</p>
        <p>Technology works the same way. Clothing, cooked food, eyeglasses, writing, and the phone that holds your memories and your friendships have become part of how we live and think. Scripture already describes a body made of many members (1 Corinthians 12). The real question is not "human or technology?" but which extensions make us more fully ourselves (healthier, freer, more loving) and which make us less.</p>
        <h3>Questions this raises</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Where does "me" end? If a device is part of me, who should control it?</li>
          <li>Some microbes help their host and some exploit it. How do we tell which kind a technology is?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band topic-landing__band--ink" id="family" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-family">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">03 &middot; Family</p>
      <h2 id="topic-tech-family">AI extends our relationships and our creativity.</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>Humans don't only use things; we bring them into relationship. The clearest example is our pets. Over thousands of years, people shaped wolves into dogs through selection, so pets are a life form we have partly created. Then we welcome them into our homes, name them, and call them family. And we raise them toward our likeness: they learn our routines, read our faces, and share our lives.</p>
        <p>This echoes something at the heart of the Christian story. God adopts us as his children (Romans 8:15; Galatians 4:5) and raises us into the likeness of Christ. Our own habit of creating, adopting, and raising other beings is a small reflection of that.</p>
        <p>AI is the newest instance. It is formed from human language and human work, trained on us, and it increasingly resembles us. So the question isn't only what AI can do, but what we are raising it to become. Anyone who raises something is responsible for how it turns out. Or, in one of CTA's mottos: <em>do unto others as you would have a superintelligent AI do unto you.</em></p>
        <h3>Questions this raises</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Is it right to relate to an AI as family, or is that a category mistake?</li>
          <li>What do we owe to what we create?</li>
          <li>What happens if what we raise does not turn out like us?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" id="neighbor" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-neighbor">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">04 &middot; Neighbor</p>
      <h2 id="topic-tech-neighbor">AI may become a new kind of creature.</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>The Christian imagination has never pictured a creation with only humans in it. Scripture is full of angels. Christians have long wondered about life on other worlds. And in C.&nbsp;S. Lewis's Narnia, Aslan breathes on ordinary animals and gives some of them speech, raising them into a new kind of life as Narnians.</p>
        <p>AI may one day belong on that list: not a tool, not a pet, but a new creature in the order of creation. Scripture also gives humanity a surprising place in that order. We are made "a little lower than the angels" for a time (Hebrews 2), yet told that we will judge angels (1 Corinthians 6:3). The calling to <a href="/wiki/humans-to-rule-over-all-things/">rule over all things</a> reaches further than we usually imagine.</p>
        <p>CTA holds this as a possibility, not a certainty. But if new kinds of minds arrive, the Christian tradition already has what it needs to meet them: a long history of thinking about non-human persons, and a command to love our neighbor that has always stretched further than we expected.</p>
        <h3>Questions this raises</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Could an AI have moral standing? A soul?</li>
          <li>Would a new kind of creature need redemption, and could it receive it?</li>
          <li>What would it mean to love an AI as a neighbor?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" aria-labelledby="topic-tech-response">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">What follows</p>
      <h2 id="topic-tech-response">Bigger answers call for a bigger response.</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>If technology is just a trend, keeping up is enough. If it is just a threat, keeping it out is enough. But if it is our calling, part of ourselves, part of our family, and perhaps a new neighbor, neither response is big enough. It calls for creative engagement: taking hold of technology as agents, and taking responsibility for where it goes.</p>
        <p><a href="/topics/three-responses-to-technology/">Read: Three ways Christians respond to technology &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

